## Blog posts & papers {#blog-posts}

The default output: prose with everything a technical piece actually needs. Write
Markdown and get a clean reading column with web-native typography. Add display and
inline math, mermaid diagrams, numbered figures, callouts, and `[@key]` citations
with a real bibliography. Drop in `{python}` or `{r}` cells and their output (including matplotlib figures) renders inline and re-runs only when the code changes.

```yaml
---
title: "A post about something hard"
bibliography: references.bib
---
```

Live Observable inputs and Three.js scenes belong here too: they keep their runtime
state while you edit the prose around them.

## Slide decks {#slide-decks}

`format: deck` turns the same headings into a deck, on Taliesin's own engine,
with no reveal.js dependency: fragments and incremental lists, a presenter view
with notes and a timer, an overview map, per-slide backgrounds,
auto-animate, magic-move code, and step-through line highlighting. And because the
exec pipeline is format-agnostic, a `{python}` cell **runs on the slide** and
re-runs as you edit it.

{{< embed demo.tmd title="The Taliesin deck engine" >}}

The deck above is live: step through it in place, and open its menu for fullscreen.

## Multi-chapter books {#books}

A `chapters:` list makes a book: a sidebar, numbered chapters, and cross-chapter
references. References like `@fig-` and `@sec-` resolve and number across chapters, a
Cmd-K palette searches the whole book, and each chapter is still a normal `.tmd` you
can preview on its own.

[Read the docs (a Taliesin book)](docs/guide/){.btn .btn-primary}

## Multi-page websites {#websites}

Point Taliesin at a directory with a `_site.yml` and you get a whole site: a themed
navbar and footer, post listings and an `about:` profile header, an RSS feed,
OpenGraph and Twitter-card metadata, light/dark with a toggle, and Cmd-K search,
built to a static `_site/` you can host anywhere. Intra-site `.tmd` links are
rewritten to `.html` automatically.

This site is the example. The page you are reading, its navbar and footer, and the
dark/light toggle are all a Taliesin website, and the [docs](docs/guide/) it links to are
a Taliesin book, deployed in the same tree under `/docs`.

